comments.body:
This is definitely a copy-and-paste spec bug for [`ReduceSumSquare`](https://onnx.ai/onnx/operators/onnx__ReduceSumSquare.html) and kin, which still incorrectly say "When axes is empty and this attribute is set to true ...":

```diff
- ...the output tensor would be equivalent to input tensor"
+ ...the output tensor would be equivalent to pow(input, 2)"
```
If you look at the [functional decomposition](https://participant4.github.io/MachineLearningOperators/OperatorFormulas.html) of each of these, you see the additional {exp, pow, log, sqrt, ...} operations occur *before* and *after* the reduction.

```
function reduceSumLog(input, axes, keepDimensions)
    return log(reduceSum(input, axes, keepDimensions)) // logₑ(x[0] + x[1] + ... + x[n-1])
endfunction

function reduceExpSumLog(input, axes, keepDimensions)
    return log(reduceSum(exp(input), axes, keepDimensions)) // logₑ(expₑ(x[0]) + expₑ(x[1]) + ... + expₑ(x[n-1]))
endfunction

function reduceSumSquare(input, axes, keepDimensions)
    return reduceSum(pow(X, 2), axes, keepDimensions) // x[0]^2 + x[1]^2 + ... + x[n-1]^2
endfunction

function reduceL1(input, axes, keepDimensions)
    return reduceSum(abs(input), axes, keepDimensions) // abs(x[0]) + abs(x[1]) + ... + abs(x[n-1])
endfunction

function reduceL2(input, axes, keepDimensions)
    return sqrt(reduceSum(pow(X, 2), axes, keepDimensions)) // sqrt(x[0]^2 + x[1]^2 + ... + x[n-1]^2)
endfunction
```

Another way to rationalize it is to ask what result would you get with an input of shape `[1,1,1,1]` (since this flag effectively turns a multaxis operation into hundreds of individual scalar operations)? The answer is that the {pow, exp, log, sqrt} *still* apply. So [`ReduceSumSquare`](https://onnx.ai/onnx/operators/onnx__ReduceSumSquare.html) *should* say: "When axes is empty and this attribute is set to true ... the output tensor would be equivalent to **the square of** the input tensor"
